        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "3a4251b9-d5c6-5971-8612-452ad0fa3654",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Myrsine, Styrax, Rapanea\n- Concepts: species counts, common names, geographic distribution, taxonomic synonymy\n\nFacts:\n- Myrsine contains about 200 species and includes notable radiations such as the matipo of New Zealand and the kōlea of Hawaiʻi.\n- The New Zealand black matipo, Pittosporum tenuifolium, is not related to Myrsine.\n- Styrax, commonly called storax or snowbell, is a genus of about 130 species of large shrubs or small trees in the family Styracaceae.\n- Styrax is mostly native to warm temperate to tropical regions of the Northern Hemisphere, with most species in eastern and southeastern Asia, and it also extends across the equator into South America.\n- Rapanea has often been treated as a synonym of Myrsine, and many Rapanea species have been transferred to Myrsine.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "61afee74-9dd3-5334-a1cc-aa61f03b0326",
        "text": "Page: Myrsine. Sentence: It contains about 200 species, including several notable radiations, such as the matipo of New Zealand and the kōlea of Hawaiʻ i (the New Zealand \"black matipo\", \"Pittosporum tenuifolium\", is not related to \"Myrsine\").\nPage: Styrax. Sentence: Storax or snowbell is the common names of Styrax, a genus of about 130 species of large shrubs or small trees in the family Styracaceae, mostly native to warm temperate to tropical regions of the Northern Hemisphere, with the majority in eastern and southeastern Asia, but also crossing the equator in South America.\nPage: Rapanea. Sentence: It has often been placed in synonymy with \"Myrsine\", and many species have been moved to \"Myrsine\".",
